  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Herbarium Specimen Sheet",
    "overview": "A run is a specimen sheet. The structure drawing lies in a double-ruled paper field like a pressed specimen; the declared result is a determination slip pinned beside it; run facts sit on a typed accession label. Nothing is ever erased. A rejected peak or a removed constraint stays on the sheet, struck through and readable, and a later confirmation arrives as a new slip on top, never mixed into the evidence. The system refuses the category default of a dark spectrum dashboard with tiles, gauges and neon traces.\n\nThe ground is cool rag paper, never cream; text and drawings are archival blue-black ink; hairlines are iron-gall sepia. Pressed-leaf olive is the one committed colour and carries the chrome (top bar, expert rail), selection and \"used\" evidence. Stamp red is reserved for critical verdicts. The night theme is a specimen cabinet: green-black ground, bone ink, the same roles. Density is that of a working document, Operate mode: one grotesque at a 1.2 scale, tabular figures everywhere, monospace only for attribution and identifiers.\n\nState is carried by line form, not hue. Every encoding of \"used, held back, removed, decisive\" reads correctly in greyscale, in forced colours and on paper.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat paper surfaces separated by sepia hairlines; one shadow in the whole system, on popovers.",
      "One committed colour (olive); red is a stamp, used on critical chips only.",
      "Line form grammar: solid used, dashed held back or open, struck removed, doubled decisive.",
      "Small-caps tracked field labels, only beside or inside the field they name.",
      "Two views, never shown together: a plain chemist view and a complete expert view."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Stamp Rule",
        "body": "Stamp red appears only on critical verdict chips. Removed or struck items use Ink 2; long-range (four bonds or more) correlations use ink plus a \"4J\" tag. If red shows anywhere else, it is a defect.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Leaf Rule",
        "body": "Olive is the only committed colour. Ochre means caution, red means critical, everything else is ink on paper.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Greyscale Test",
        "body": "Print the page in greyscale: every state must still read, because line form carries it and hue only reinforces it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Typed Label Rule",
        "body": "Small-caps tracked labels sit only beside or inside the field they name, like typed accession labels. Never above a heading as a category tag.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Determination Rule",
        "body": "The slip's heading is the result in plain words. Attribution follows below it as one monospace \"Det.\" line (who determined it, scope); no label sits above the heading.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Small-Caps Symbol Rule",
        "body": "Greek symbols inside small-caps headers opt out of case mapping, so δ never becomes Δ.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Views Rule",
        "body": "Chemist and expert views are never shown together. One switch in the top bar and \"why?\" links move between them.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper Stack Rule",
        "body": "A surface is lighter the closer it is to the reader: sunk, then paper, then slip. Nothing floats except the popover.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do carry every state by line form first: solid used, dashed held back or open, struck removed, doubled decisive.",
      "Do keep struck items readable in Ink 2; nothing disappears, it cancels.",
      "Do keep stamp red (stamp-red) on critical verdict chips only.",
      "Do draw positive 2D contours in contour-positive and negative ones dotted in contour-negative; mark curated peaks in olive-line with a paper halo.",
      "Do make the slip heading the determination itself, with the monospace \"Det.\" line below it.",
      "Do give atom numbers and correlation arrows a paper halo or underlay so they read over each other.",
      "Do pair every canvas with a table and every colour with a shape, line form or word.",
      "Do keep the two views separate and link them with \"why?\"."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use red for removed, struck or long-range items; use Ink 2, or ink with a \"4J\" tag.",
      "Don't put a small-caps label or category tag above a heading; field labels live beside their fields.",
      "Don't write solver notation in the chemist view; the narrowing is plain numbers.",
      "Don't add shadows to cards, slips or registers; depth is paper tone and hairline.",
      "Don't introduce a second committed colour, gradients, neon traces, gauges or tiles.",
      "Don't use cream or warm grounds; the paper is cool grey-white.",
      "Don't show the chemist and expert views side by side."
    ]
  }
}